 ### General Radiance Field Rendering via MLP
-General Radiance Field Rendering via MLP is an MLP that predicts RGB values and volumetric density for a 3D point using its aggregated feature vector and viewing direction, forming a general radiance field [S11]. The steps are: Render an image pixel RGB by casting a ray from the camera center and estimating the integral using volume rendering equations with numerical quadrature [S11]. Estimate the integrals with numerical quadrature using a hierarchical sampling strategy, strictly following NeRF [S11]. All 3D locations and RGB values are processed by the positional encoding proposed by NeRF [S11]. Optimization uses L2 loss between rendered RGBs and ground truth [S11]. Unlike original NeRF which uses point position xyz as input, this method uses the aggregated point features $\bar{F}_p$ as input to the MLP [S11].
+General Radiance Field Rendering via MLP is an MLP that predicts RGB values and volumetric density for a 3D point using its aggregated feature vector and viewing direction, forming a general radiance field [S6]. The steps are: Render an image pixel RGB by casting a ray from the camera center and estimating the integral using volume rendering equations with numerical quadrature [S6]. Estimate the integrals with numerical quadrature using a hierarchical sampling strategy, strictly following NeRF [S6]. All 3D locations and RGB values are processed by the positional encoding proposed by NeRF [S6]. Optimization uses L2 loss between rendered RGBs and ground truth [S6]. Unlike original NeRF which uses point position xyz as input, this method uses the aggregated point features $\bar{F}_p$ as input to the MLP [S6].
 
-### Baking Implicit NeRF into Multi-resolution Voxel Mesh
-Baking Implicit NeRF into Multi-resolution Voxel Mesh is a technique to replace the computational bottleneck of MLP inference in implicit NeRF by pre-calculating voxel properties (color and density) into a multi-resolution voxel grid structure [S12]. The steps are: Spatial division: Divide scene space into a three-level voxel grid based on adaptive resolution. Define voxel side length $s_k = \frac{S}{2^k}$ where $S$ is the maximum side length of the scene and $k=0, 1, 2$ correspond to LOD0 (512³), LOD1 (256³), and LOD2 (128³) [S12].
+### Semantic Pseudo Label with Global Structure Prior
+Semantic Pseudo Label with Global Structure Prior uses a global structure prior using a pre-trained Vision Transformer (ViT) to enforce semantic consistency between unseen views and the reference view, robust to pixel-wise misalignment [S7]. Unlike DietNeRF which uses projected image embeddings, directly extract the [CLS] token from DINO-ViT’s output as it serves as a representation of an entire image [S7]. Calculate L2 distance between extracted features: $\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}} = ||f_{\text{vit}}(A) - f_{\text{vit}}(B) ||^2$, where $f_{vit}(\cdot)$ refers to the extracted [CLS] tokens, and A and B are patches from the reference view and an unseen view, respectively [S7]. The method adopts DINO-ViT [4], a self-supervised vision transformer trained on ImageNet [8] dataset [S7].
+
+### iVR-GS
+iVR-GS is a method for real-time editing of transfer functions and lighting during neural volume rendering using editable and composable 3DGS primitives, overcoming the limitation of baked visual parameters [S8, unofficial]. Steps: 1. Compose multiple basic models trained on disjoint transfer function ranges. 2. Extend Gaussian primitives with normals and shading coefficients. 3. Incorporate Blinn-Phong reflection. 4. Enable real-time editing of color, opacity, and lighting at inference. 5. Perform automatic optimization to approximate reference images [S8, unofficial]. Pitfalls: Model size scales linearly with the number of basic scenes, reaching gigabyte-level for hundreds of transfer functions. Uses uniform viewpoint sampling which may be suboptimal. Requires redesigning lighting representation for models beyond Blinn-Phong [S8, unofficial]. Reconstruction quality: PSNR approximately 32–34 dB for novel view synthesis, 30–33 dB for relighting [S8, unofficial]. Compression: Vector quantization achieved 7.2× compression (6.2 MB vs. 44.8 MB) while maintaining accuracy within 0.06 dB [S8, unofficial].
+
+### R2L
+R2L is a method distilling neural radiance fields into neural light fields via a deep residual MLP to improve rendering efficiency [S9]. Train by distilling from a pre-trained NeRF [S9]. Achieves 26–35× reduction in FLOPs [S9]. Achieves 28–31× speedup in wall-clock time [S9].
+
+### EG3D
+EG3D is a GAN framework using a novel hybrid tri-plane representation for neural rendering, achieving state-of-the-art results on FFHQ [S9].
+
+### Radiance-Driven Fluid Simulation
+Radiance-Driven Fluid Simulation is a joint training framework where the fluid simulator is learned from video supervision by backpropagating the error between rendered images and ground-truth video frames through the differentiable renderer to the simulator [S10, unofficial]. Steps: Advect the volume field $V_t$ using the velocity field $u_t$ inferred by the simulator to get the updated volume field $V_{t+1}$ [S10, unofficial]. Assign the updated volume field $V_{t+1}$ into the scene space to render image $\hat{I}_{t+1}$ [S10, unofficial]. Supervise the rendered image $\hat{I}_{t+1}$ with the ground-truth image $I_{t+1}$ to update simulator parameters [S10, unofficial]. Pre-train the radiance field network with the initial volume field of all training sequences (volume of first frames) before joint training [S10, unofficial]. Formulas: $V_{t+1} = \text{advect}(u_t, \Delta t, V_t)$ [S10, unofficial]. $L = \sum_r \|\hat{C}(r) - C(r)\|_2^2 + \beta \|\nabla \cdot u\|_2^2$, where $\beta$ controls regularization strength [S10, unofficial]. Parameter: $\beta$: Regularization strength for divergence penalty, set to $1e-6$ [S10, unofficial].
+
+### Online Learning for Irradiance Volumes
+Online Learning for Irradiance Volumes updates a learned irradiance cache at runtime, extending surface-based neural caching to full volumetric scene representations [S11]. Steps: Initialize online learning from a previously learned NIV to significantly improve convergence compared to learning from scratch [S11]. To achieve real-time updates, restrict sampling to the camera's view frustum and/or employ loss-driven sampling [DPD22] [S11]. Pitfall: Inherently slower to learn than surface methods due to a larger input domain (entire scene volume vs. surface subset) and the requirement for a low-bias representation to avoid deferring queries [S11].
+
+### 3DGS Radiance Caching
+3DGS Radiance Caching is a caching technique for path-traced volume rendering that uses 3DGS primitives to store attenuated radiance at multiple path lengths, optimizing exponentially subsampled Gaussians from noisy path samples to achieve real-time frame rates [S8, unofficial]. Parameter: Memory footprint: 28 MB for the cache, compared to 272 MB for neural radiance caching [S8, unofficial]. Pitfall: Introduces bias with no guaranteed error bounds and requires re-initialization when transfer functions change [S8, unofficial]. Performance on six scientific datasets at 720p: real-time frame rates achieved with 38–186ms total time [S8, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Radiance-field optimization emerged as a dominant paradigm for novel view synthesis following the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2]. Prior to this, image-based rendering relied on light-field sampling or explicit geometry reconstruction [S2]. NeRF demonstrated that a continuous volumetric function, optimized via gradient descent to minimize photometric error, could synthesize photorealistic images from sparse views [S1, unofficial][S5][S10]. This approach leveraged the differentiability of the volume rendering equation, allowing gradients to flow from pixel space to the neural network weights [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. The field has since expanded to include explicit representations like voxel grids [4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md] and Gaussian splats [1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md], as well as applications in dynamic scenes [912-neural-volumes.md], medical imaging [S5], and non-optical domains like radar [8904-radar-fields.md].
